  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 218 AM CDT Sat Aug 1 2026\n\nEarly this morning, water vapor satellite and upper air analysis \nshowed an upper low centered across far southeast IA. A broad upper \nridge was located across the southwest US with a ridge axis extending\nnorthward across the central Rockies. An upper level trough was \nlocated off the Pacific Northwest coastline.\n\nThe 6Z surface observations showed a surface low across western IL, \nwith a cold front extending southward across  across western IL, then \nsouthwest across southern OK into the South Plains of TX. A surface \nridge of  high pressure extended southward from the northern Plains, \nsouthward across central NE, into west central KS. Stratus cloud \ncover was shifting soth-southwest across far eastern KS, around an \n850mb low across far northeast MO. temperatures across the northern \ncounties were already down into the middle 60s, with upper 60s to \naround 70 degrees across the remainder of the area. Dewpoints were \nin the upper 50s to lower 60s.\n\nToday through Tuesday:\n\nAfter some morning clouds across northeast and east central KS, \nskies will become sunny as the h5 trough digs southeast into \nsouthern IL. The center of the surface ridge will build southward \ninto west central KS through the day. During the mid morning hours, \nthe PBL will mix deeper and transport down some higher momentum air \nparcels. Thus, the north winds should increase to 15 to 25 MPH with \nwind gusts of 25 to 35 MPH during the late morning and afternoon \nhours. The surface winds will diminish late this afternoon into the \nearly evening hours. Highs will only reach the lower to mid 80s, and\nsurface dewpoints will be in the lower to mid 60s.\n\nThe H5 trough across the Pacific northwest will shift east into the \nnorthern high Plains by 00Z TUE. The H5 ridge across the southwest \nUS will build east into the central and southern Highs Plains by \nMonday afternoon.\n\nSunday will continue to be nice, with highs in the lower to mid 80s \nas the center of the surface ridge shifts east across the CWA. \n\nMonday will be warmer with the H5 ridge expanding east into the \ncentral Plains. HIghs on Monday will reach the upper 80s east to \nlower 90s west. Surface dewpoints may increase a bit as surface \nwinds become southeasterly Monday afternoon.\n\nTuesday, H5 trough across the northern high Plains will shifts east-\nsoutheast with the southern portions of the H5 trough moving east \nacross NE. A weak front will slowly push south across NE. We should \nremain dry with thunderstorms remaining north across NE. Highs will \nreach the upper 80s to lower 90s. There may be s few mid 90 degree \nreadings across the southwest counties. \n\nTuesday night through Saturday:\n\nThe H5 ridge will retrograde westward into the west central US. The \nmid level flow across the Plain will become more northwesterly. The \nextend range model solutions are in fair agreement. The GFS model \nsolution is a bit farther south with the H5 trough Tuesday night \ninto Wednesday. The ECMWF and Canadian models show the H5 trough not\ndigging much farther south then NE, where most the QPF Tuesday night\ninto Wednesday remains north of the CWA. \n\nThe storms across NE Tuesday will help to push a front southward \nacross the CWA late Tuesday night. A complex of storms may form \nacross central NE Tuesday night and move southeast across portions \nof northeast KS during the morning hours of Wednesday, according to \nthe GFS model. If the GFS solution verifies, any complex of storms \nmay provide northeast and east central KS with potential heavy \nrainfall and gusty winds. It is a little too early to determine if \nthe complex of storms will produce severe wind gusts, and the exact \ntrack of any complex of thunderstorms. Plus the ECMWF and Canadian\nmodels keep the complex of storms north of the CWA across NE.\n\nHIghs on Wednesday will be cooler north of I-70 with lower to mid \n80s, while areas along and south of I-70 will be in the lower to mid \n90s. This temperature forecast could change depending on where the \nfront and outflow boundary become stationary across the CWA. \nWednesday afternoon additional showers and thunderstorms will develop \nalong and north of the boundary Wednesday afternoon into the\novernight hours. The effective shear will be around 30 KTS and the \nMLCAPE will be 1500-2500 J/KG. Once again there could be a few severe\nthunderstorms. Another complex of storms may develop Wednesday night\nacross southeast NE and northeast KS.\n\nThursday through Saturday:\n\nEmbedded perturbations in the west-northwest flow may provide enough \nascent for isolated to widely scattered thunderstorms through the \nperiod. The front will be nearly stationary across the CWA. The \nfront will begin to lift northward into southern NE by Saturday. \nHighs will reach the lower to mid 90s.",
